WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.770
It lasts a thousandth of a second, travels billions of light years, and in a single flash releases as much energy as the Sun puts out in a day.

2
00:00:08.770 --> 00:00:17.700
For years nobody knew where these things came from — on one occasion the source of the signal turned out to be the microwave oven in the observatory kitchen.

3
00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:25.030
But the real ones achieved something remarkable: they weighed the universe's long-missing matter and showed us where it was hiding.

4
00:00:33.230 --> 00:00:42.320
It lasts one thousandth of a second, it has travelled billions of light years, and in a single flash it dumps out as much energy as the Sun radiates in a day.

5
00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:51.730
For years nobody could say where these things came from — on one memorable occasion the source of the signal turned out to be the microwave oven in an observatory kitchen.

6
00:00:51.730 --> 00:00:59.700
But the genuine ones managed something remarkable: they weighed the matter the universe had been hiding for decades, and showed us where it was.

7
00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:07.350
One of the drearier jobs in astronomy is going back over data that other people looked at years ago and moved on from.

8
00:01:07.350 --> 00:01:17.080
In two thousand seven, at West Virginia University, Duncan Lorimer handed exactly that job to an undergraduate named David Narkevic.

9
00:01:17.080 --> 00:01:27.770
What they had was an archive collected years earlier with the sixty-four metre dish of the Parkes Observatory, in the Australian state of New South Wales, and the task was to sift through it for overlooked,

10
00:01:27.770 --> 00:01:35.260
isolated pulses from pulsars — neutron stars that spin many times a second and send out regular radio beats.

11
00:01:35.260 --> 00:01:39.070
Nobody was expecting a new class of celestial object.

12
00:01:39.070 --> 00:01:41.600
It was a matter of patience.

13
00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:48.610
In a recording dated the twenty-fourth of August, two thousand one, Narkevic ran into something he had not been looking for.

14
00:01:48.610 --> 00:01:56.580
While the telescope was pointed near the edge of the Small Magellanic Cloud, there had been a flash lasting less than five milliseconds.

15
00:01:56.580 --> 00:02:03.270
Brevity aside, the truly odd thing was its brightness: strong enough to swamp everything else in that patch of sky.

16
00:02:03.270 --> 00:02:05.640
And it had never repeated.

17
00:02:05.640 --> 00:02:11.210
But what made the signal genuinely interesting was not its brightness — it was its shape.

18
00:02:11.210 --> 00:02:18.060
Radio telescopes do not listen to the sky at one single frequency; they listen across an entire band at once.

19
00:02:18.060 --> 00:02:24.750
This flash reached the top of the band, the high frequencies, first, and arrived at the low frequencies later.

20
00:02:24.750 --> 00:02:34.960
The lower you went, the larger the delay, and not in a haphazard way but with mathematical cleanliness: the delay was inversely proportional to the square of the frequency.

21
00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:37.970
The reason for this is well understood.

22
00:02:37.970 --> 00:02:45.780
The space between the stars is not empty; it holds an extraordinarily thin plasma of free electrons torn away from their nuclei.

23
00:02:45.780 --> 00:02:52.790
As radio waves cross that plasma they slow down slightly, and low frequencies slow a little more than high ones.

24
00:02:52.790 --> 00:03:00.280
The longer the path, the more electrons the wave has to pass through, and the wider the spread in arrival times.

25
00:03:00.280 --> 00:03:09.370
Astronomers measure that spread with a single number: the dispersion measure, which is essentially a count of how many electrons the beam crossed along its journey.

26
00:03:09.370 --> 00:03:13.500
For this burst, that number was three hundred and seventy-five.

27
00:03:13.500 --> 00:03:19.390
Roughly fifteen times what the models predicted the Milky Way ought to contribute in that direction.

28
00:03:19.390 --> 00:03:26.400
In other words, the signal had passed through far more layers of matter than the plasma of our own galaxy could possibly account for.

29
00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:33.570
There was only one reasonable conclusion: the source lay outside the Milky Way, probably billions of light years away.

30
00:03:33.570 --> 00:03:41.540
And if that was right, then within a few milliseconds that source had released a burst of radio energy approaching what the Sun emits over days.

31
00:03:41.540 --> 00:03:50.310
The delay between the top and the bottom of the band came close to a quarter of a second; that quarter of a second was the signature of the distance the signal had covered.

32
00:03:50.310 --> 00:03:55.720
The astronomical community did not believe it straight away, and they were right not to.

33
00:03:55.720 --> 00:04:00.170
One telescope, one event, a recording with no repeat.

34
00:04:00.170 --> 00:04:06.860
What is more, the same observatory's data had for years contained another strange family of signals.

35
00:04:06.860 --> 00:04:12.270
These too lasted milliseconds, and these too spread out with frequency.

36
00:04:12.270 --> 00:04:20.720
They had been given the name perytons, after a mythical creature that was part stag and part bird, and the choice was apt, because nobody knew what they were.

37
00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:24.370
The perytons had two unsettling properties.

38
00:04:24.370 --> 00:04:30.580
First, their dispersion measures were always close to one another, clustering around four hundred and ten.

39
00:04:30.580 --> 00:04:36.150
There is no reason for signals arriving from every corner of the universe to agree on a number like that.

40
00:04:36.150 --> 00:04:45.240
Second, and worse: the observatory's receiver watched the sky with thirteen separate beams, and the perytons turned up in every one of them at once.

41
00:04:45.240 --> 00:04:49.850
Light coming from a point on the sky falls into only one or two beams.

42
00:04:49.850 --> 00:04:54.460
Something that arrives from everywhere at once is not coming from the sky.

43
00:04:54.460 --> 00:04:58.750
Suspicion deepened, because the perytons were fond of office hours.

44
00:04:58.750 --> 00:05:02.240
They clustered on weekdays, around the middle of the day.

45
00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:12.130
In two thousand fifteen, a team including Emily Petroff installed a monitor at the observatory to keep continuous watch on electromagnetic interference at the site.

46
00:05:12.130 --> 00:05:21.060
In January of that year three perytons were caught, and sitting right on top of each one was a signal blazing at around two point four gigahertz.

47
00:05:21.060 --> 00:05:25.030
That is the operating frequency of microwave ovens.

48
00:05:25.030 --> 00:05:28.680
The source was the observatory kitchen.

49
00:05:28.680 --> 00:05:38.730
When a microwave door is opened before the timer runs out, the magnetron inside is still winding down, and for an instant it leaks a short burst of radio energy into the room.

50
00:05:38.730 --> 00:05:43.500
The team was able to reproduce the signal over and over by doing it deliberately.

51
00:05:43.500 --> 00:05:49.550
Some of the mysterious bursts that had occupied astronomers for years were coming from reheated lunches.

52
00:05:49.550 --> 00:05:54.800
But this discovery did not discredit the real bursts; if anything it cleaned them up.

53
00:05:54.800 --> 00:06:03.410
Because the way the perytons spread across frequency did not quite obey the physical law that governs plasma, whereas the real bursts fitted it perfectly.

54
00:06:03.410 --> 00:06:08.180
Perytons fell into all of the beams; the real bursts into only one.

55
00:06:08.180 --> 00:06:18.870
And in November of two thousand twelve, on the other side of the world, the Arecibo Observatory in Puerto Rico caught a burst in a single beam, with a clean frequency sweep,

56
00:06:18.870 --> 00:06:22.680
and a dispersion measure of five hundred and fifty-seven.

57
00:06:22.680 --> 00:06:27.290
Local noise cannot imitate the same physics in two hemispheres at once.

58
00:06:27.290 --> 00:06:31.100
Today we call these events fast radio bursts.

59
00:06:31.100 --> 00:06:41.630
The CHIME telescope in Canada, the Canadian Hydrogen Intensity Mapping Experiment, which came online in two thousand eighteen and sweeps the sky with fixed half-cylindrical antennas,

60
00:06:41.630 --> 00:06:48.000
listed more than five hundred bursts in its first catalogue, and the number has run into the thousands since.

61
00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:54.530
Estimates suggest that across the whole sky, thousands of fast radio bursts go off every single day.

62
00:06:54.530 --> 00:06:58.340
So they are not rare; they are simply very short.

63
00:06:58.340 --> 00:07:03.910
Once you accept that a signal is real, a much harder question begins: what is making it?

64
00:07:03.910 --> 00:07:06.600
Duration is a brutal constraint here.

65
00:07:06.600 --> 00:07:14.890
If an object brightens and fades in less than a millisecond, then light must be able to cross from one end of that object to the other in less than a millisecond.

66
00:07:14.890 --> 00:07:20.300
Otherwise the flash would be blurred out by the delays coming from different parts of the source.

67
00:07:20.300 --> 00:07:24.590
Light travels three hundred kilometres in a millisecond.

68
00:07:24.590 --> 00:07:34.800
So the thing that hurls out, in a single instant and from billions of light years away, as much energy as the Sun produces in days, is roughly the size of a city.

69
00:07:34.800 --> 00:07:45.170
What is more, this radiation cannot be the kind a hot body gives off as heat; the temperature you would need for such brightness corresponds to no meaningful value in physics.

70
00:07:45.170 --> 00:07:49.140
That leaves only one option: coherent emission.

71
00:07:49.140 --> 00:07:58.070
Charged particles oscillating not one by one, but in vast swarms, in step with one another, stacking their radio waves on top of each other.

72
00:07:58.070 --> 00:08:03.640
And we know of one place in nature that can do this: extreme magnetic fields.

73
00:08:03.640 --> 00:08:06.330
Which brings us to magnetars.

74
00:08:06.330 --> 00:08:12.380
A magnetar is an extreme kind of neutron star, what remains of the collapsed core of a massive star.

75
00:08:12.380 --> 00:08:22.270
It is about twenty kilometres across, close to one and a half times the mass of the Sun, and a single teaspoon of its material would weigh hundreds of millions of tonnes on Earth.

76
00:08:22.270 --> 00:08:30.400
Its defining feature is its magnetic field: trillions of times stronger than Earth's, the most violent magnetic structure in the known universe.

77
00:08:30.400 --> 00:08:34.210
This field squeezes the star's crust like a vice.

78
00:08:34.210 --> 00:08:41.860
The crust of a neutron star is a layer in which atomic nuclei are locked into crystalline order, incomparably harder than steel.

79
00:08:41.860 --> 00:08:50.470
But as the magnetic field decays over time it bends the crust, stretches it, and eventually loads it with more stress than it can carry.

80
00:08:50.470 --> 00:09:02.120
The result is a starquake: the crust cracks, the magnetic field lines anchored to that crack lash outward like whips, reconnect, and fling out waves of charged particles at close to the speed of light.

81
00:09:02.120 --> 00:09:07.530
At the shock front these waves create, coherent radio emission can be produced.

82
00:09:07.530 --> 00:09:11.340
For a long time this was a plausible scenario on paper.

83
00:09:11.340 --> 00:09:15.310
In April of two thousand twenty it turned into evidence.

84
00:09:15.310 --> 00:09:28.080
In the direction of the constellation Vulpecula, inside the Milky Way, some thirty thousand light years from us, a magnetar named SGR nineteen thirty-five plus twenty-one fifty-four had turned restless in those days;

85
00:09:28.080 --> 00:09:32.530
space telescopes were seeing X-ray bursts from it one after another.

86
00:09:32.530 --> 00:09:41.140
On the twenty-eighth of April, in the middle of that X-ray storm, came a radio burst short enough to last a single millisecond.

87
00:09:41.140 --> 00:09:47.670
The Canadian telescope caught it from outside its main field of view, through a side lobe of its sensitivity.

88
00:09:47.670 --> 00:09:56.760
At the same moment a second system in the United States, built from a handful of small antennas that look almost primitive on their own, recorded the very same burst.

89
00:09:56.760 --> 00:10:03.450
And within that same second, several space telescopes saw a hard X-ray flash from the magnetar.

90
00:10:03.450 --> 00:10:07.900
The timing was far too precise to be left to coincidence.

91
00:10:07.900 --> 00:10:13.310
This was the first fast radio burst to come not from beyond our galaxy but from within it.

92
00:10:13.310 --> 00:10:19.200
Its power was about thirty times weaker than even the faintest extragalactic burst seen up to that day.

93
00:10:19.200 --> 00:10:23.970
Yet it was still far stronger than anything an ordinary pulsar could come close to.

94
00:10:23.970 --> 00:10:29.220
So magnetars, even if only at the lower end of the scale, could do the job.

95
00:10:29.220 --> 00:10:34.150
From here on it is still open-ended, and that has to be said honestly.

96
00:10:34.150 --> 00:10:42.600
Some of the bursts repeat; the source of the event the Arecibo Observatory caught flared again, over and over, in two thousand sixteen.

97
00:10:42.600 --> 00:10:45.130
Others never come back at all.

98
00:10:45.130 --> 00:10:52.460
Whether these two groups come from the same kind of object, or represent two separate physical processes, is still debated.

99
00:10:52.460 --> 00:11:04.110
And so is the question of exactly where the emission is generated: inside the magnetar's magnetosphere, or much further out, at the shock front where the ejected material slams into the surrounding medium?

100
00:11:04.110 --> 00:11:07.120
There are no answers to these questions yet.

101
00:11:07.120 --> 00:11:12.690
There is one more thing missing: any evidence whatsoever in favour of an artificial source.

102
00:11:12.690 --> 00:11:21.620
When the bursts were discovered that possibility was raised as well, but the frequency sweep of the signals follows the law of natural plasma to the letter,

103
00:11:21.620 --> 00:11:27.350
and the sources are scattered in every direction across the sky and through different depths of the universe.

104
00:11:27.350 --> 00:11:30.200
These are natural phenomena.

105
00:11:30.200 --> 00:11:35.130
And in any case, what comes after this is the truly astonishing part.

106
00:11:35.130 --> 00:11:40.860
Up to now, with these bursts, we have been looking at the carrier — at the signal itself.

107
00:11:40.860 --> 00:11:50.110
And yet the real place fast radio bursts hold in the history of science lies not in the message they carry, but in what happens to them along the way.

108
00:11:50.110 --> 00:11:54.400
To see this, we need to look at the universe's ledger.

109
00:11:54.400 --> 00:12:01.410
Roughly sixty-eight percent of the universe's energy content is dark energy, and twenty-seven percent is dark matter.

110
00:12:01.410 --> 00:12:06.980
What is left, about five percent, is the matter we know: protons and neutrons.

111
00:12:06.980 --> 00:12:10.790
Stars, planets, gas, us.

112
00:12:10.790 --> 00:12:13.960
That five percent figure is not a guess.

113
00:12:13.960 --> 00:12:24.810
Both the abundances of the light elements forged in the first three minutes and the measured patterns of the cosmic microwave background arrive at the very same number, by two completely independent routes.

114
00:12:24.810 --> 00:12:30.220
The problem was this: when astronomers tried to count that matter, they could not find it.

115
00:12:30.220 --> 00:12:42.510
When they added up the stars in the nearby universe, the gas inside galaxies, the hot gas in galaxy clusters, the clouds drifting between galaxies, they came away with only about half of what should have been there.

116
00:12:42.510 --> 00:12:45.840
This became known as the missing baryon problem.

117
00:12:45.840 --> 00:12:49.330
Note carefully: this is not dark matter.

118
00:12:49.330 --> 00:12:54.100
Dark matter is a separate puzzle, and it is already accounted for in the tally.

119
00:12:54.100 --> 00:12:59.190
What went missing here is ordinary matter, whose nature we understand perfectly well.

120
00:12:59.190 --> 00:13:02.200
We simply did not know where it was.

121
00:13:02.200 --> 00:13:04.410
Theory had an answer ready.

122
00:13:04.410 --> 00:13:14.460
This matter had to be sitting outside the galaxies, inside the filaments that weave the universe together like a web, heated to somewhere between one hundred thousand and ten million degrees,

123
00:13:14.460 --> 00:13:17.950
in the form of an extraordinarily thin gas.

124
00:13:17.950 --> 00:13:29.600
But that puts the gas in precisely the hardest state to see: hot enough to emit X-rays, yet not dense enough to emit a measurable amount of them, and not substantial enough to leave an absorption line either.

125
00:13:29.600 --> 00:13:35.330
For decades, the efforts to reveal this gas hovered right at the edge of detectability.

126
00:13:35.330 --> 00:13:39.300
And here is where the gift of fast radio bursts comes in.

127
00:13:39.300 --> 00:13:43.430
The dispersion measure does not care about the temperature of the gas.

128
00:13:43.430 --> 00:13:48.840
It does not care how thin it is, what phase it is in, or whether it radiates at all.

129
00:13:48.840 --> 00:13:55.210
It counts one thing and one thing only: how many free electrons lie along the path of the beam.

130
00:13:55.210 --> 00:14:01.100
A fast radio burst weighs the entire column of matter stretching from its source to us, all at once.

131
00:14:01.100 --> 00:14:04.910
Including the part we cannot see.

132
00:14:04.910 --> 00:14:08.560
Only one thing was missing: distance.

133
00:14:08.560 --> 00:14:15.730
On its own, the dispersion measure cannot distinguish thin gas along a long path from dense gas along a short one.

134
00:14:15.730 --> 00:14:21.140
To solve the equation, you had to know exactly where the burst came from.

135
00:14:21.140 --> 00:14:24.150
And for that a single dish is not enough.

136
00:14:24.150 --> 00:14:35.320
You have to run antennas separated by kilometres in concert, pin down the burst's position to within an arcsecond, then find the galaxy sitting at that spot, take its spectrum with an optical telescope,

137
00:14:35.320 --> 00:14:38.650
and read off its distance from the redshift.

138
00:14:38.650 --> 00:14:44.220
In May of two thousand twenty, a team led by Jean-Pierre Macquart pulled this off.

139
00:14:44.220 --> 00:14:54.590
The radio array in Western Australia, ASKAP, the Australian Square Kilometre Array Pathfinder, had delivered a handful of bursts whose positions they could pin down.

140
00:14:54.590 --> 00:15:00.480
For each one, the team laid two numbers side by side: the dispersion measure and the redshift.

141
00:15:00.480 --> 00:15:03.810
The relationship that emerged was simple.

142
00:15:03.810 --> 00:15:08.260
The more distant the burst, the more electrons its signal had passed through.

143
00:15:08.260 --> 00:15:16.070
The slope of that line gave, directly and immediately, the average density of ordinary matter in intergalactic space.

144
00:15:16.070 --> 00:15:21.160
The result came out at around five percent of the total energy content of the universe.

145
00:15:21.160 --> 00:15:26.410
In other words, exactly the number the cosmic microwave background had given.

146
00:15:26.410 --> 00:15:35.980
The missing baryons were not missing at all; they were sitting in the void between the galaxies, precisely where theory said they would be, and now they had been weighed.

147
00:15:35.980 --> 00:15:46.190
Today this relationship is known as the Macquart relation, because the astronomer who gave it his name died unexpectedly in two thousand twenty, very shortly after the paper was published.

148
00:15:46.190 --> 00:15:50.000
What is truly lovely is that the story does not stop there.

149
00:15:50.000 --> 00:15:57.330
The scatter around the relation, the deviation of individual bursts from the average line, looked at first like a margin of error.

150
00:15:57.330 --> 00:15:59.220
It is not.

151
00:15:59.220 --> 00:16:03.510
That scatter is the lumpy structure of the cosmic web itself.

152
00:16:03.510 --> 00:16:10.520
If a burst's beam passed through a dense filament, its delay grows; if it crossed a void, the delay shrinks.

153
00:16:10.520 --> 00:16:17.370
Accumulate enough bursts, and those deviations turn into a map of how matter between the galaxies is clustered.

154
00:16:17.370 --> 00:16:27.900
And indeed, work in recent years has gathered enough data to show that the matter outside galaxies is not scattered at random; it favours the regions where galaxy density runs high.

155
00:16:27.900 --> 00:16:31.870
The same method reaches toward a finer question too.

156
00:16:31.870 --> 00:16:39.680
If a burst's beam grazes the edge of a foreground galaxy along its path, there is an unexpected jump in the dispersion measure.

157
00:16:39.680 --> 00:16:45.730
The size of that jump tells you how full the halo of gas is that the galaxy has flung out around itself.

158
00:16:45.730 --> 00:16:54.660
How much matter galaxies blow outward through the supernovae and black holes inside them has been one of the hardest quantities in galaxy evolution to measure.

159
00:16:54.660 --> 00:17:00.230
Now, with flashes lasting a millisecond, those halos can be probed one by one.

160
00:17:00.230 --> 00:17:03.240
The scale is growing too.

161
00:17:03.240 --> 00:17:14.090
In two thousand twenty-three, the Australian array caught a burst with a redshift greater than one; its light had travelled for nearly eight billion years before reaching us, and the relation held there as well.

162
00:17:14.090 --> 00:17:25.420
Today the number of bursts whose host galaxy has been pinned down runs to a few dozen, but with the new generation of arrays that number is expected to reach the hundreds, and then the thousands, before long.

163
00:17:25.420 --> 00:17:35.150
Once enough examples have accumulated, these bursts could become a tool not only for mapping where the matter is, but for measuring the expansion rate of the universe by an independent route.

164
00:17:35.150 --> 00:17:41.200
Notice this: what makes all of it possible is not the brightness or the power of the burst.

165
00:17:41.200 --> 00:17:43.730
It is the corruption of the signal.

166
00:17:43.730 --> 00:17:54.100
That delay it picks up along the way, the one that plagued astronomers at the outset, that smeared the signal, that was treated as a flaw to be corrected for in archive searches.

167
00:17:54.100 --> 00:18:02.710
The scale that weighs the invisible matter of the universe turned out to be the quarter of a second a millisecond-long flash loses between one frequency and another.

168
00:18:02.710 --> 00:18:08.440
In science, the thing you want to measure is sometimes hidden inside the noise you are trying to clean away.

169
00:18:08.440 --> 00:18:12.250
Nöron Science'de bugünkü bölümün sonuna geldik.

170
00:18:12.250 --> 00:18:15.580
Yeni bir hikâyede yeniden buluşmak üzere.
